            <ArticleTitle>Burkitt’s Lymphoma: Thorax to Pelvis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>49-51</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Burkitt’s lymphoma is a sub-group of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma of high-grade with an aggressive clinical course and is composed of diffuse, small and non-cleaved, undifferentiated malignant cells of lymphoid origin. Dennis Burkitt first described this entity in 1956 in equatorial Africa. It is one of the fastest growing cancers in humans with a growth fraction close to 100%. It commonly occurs in children and young adults, with frequent involvement of bone marrow and central nervous system. These are considered to be medical emergencies and require immediate diagnostic and therapeutic intervention. In this report, we present a case of Burkitt’s lymphoma with unusual presentation with the involvement of both thorax and the whole of the abdomen.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
